<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.8.3: Finding and Interpreting Average Rates of Change</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00985</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.8.3: Finding and Interpreting Average Rates of Change</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6e9e8c79-af8d-4b92-afef-4fc605ae5155</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>The graphs show the populations of California and Texas over time.</para>
<image alt="Graph of the populations of two states over time on a coordinate plane. " src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/127fe8074872a6c9489127ecd8679d6c4f86ec31"/><exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Estimate the average rate of change in the population in California between 1970 and 2010. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The population of California was about 20 million in 1970 and 37 million in 2010. The average rate of change is: \(\frac{37-20}{2010-1970}=\frac{17}{40}=0.425\) million per year.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>In this situation, what does the rate of change mean for California?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>On average, the population of California was growing by 425,000 people per year.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Estimate the average rate of change in the population in Texas between 1970 and 2010. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The population of Texas was about 11 million in 1970 and 25 million in 2010. The average rate of change is \(\frac{25-11}{2010-1970}=\frac{14}{40}=0.35\) million per year.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>In this situation, what does the rate of change mean for Texas?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>On average, the population of Texas was growing by 350,000 people per year.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Which state's population grew more quickly between 1900 and 2000? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>California is growing more quickly. For example: If we connect each pair of points representing the population of each state in 1900 and 2000, the segment for California has a greater slope, so it has a greater average rate of change.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
